.TH OPENFORENSIC-IMAGER 8 "2026-11-01" "OpenForensic Imager 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
openforensic-imager \- Linux-native forensic acquisition and evidence analysis
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B openforensic-imager
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B openforensic-imager
is a graphical workbench for acquiring and analysing digital evidence. It
creates and verifies RAW and EWF1 images, analyses MBR and GPT partition
tables, browses supported filesystems through The Sleuth Kit, and records case
metadata and audit events.
.PP
The application is controlled through its window; the options below exist for
scripted checks and troubleshooting. Evidence-facing operations do not run in
this process. Each one is dispatched to
.BR ofi-parser-worker (8),
which opens the evidence and returns the result.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-self\-test
Load the interface, confirm that the QML scene builds, and exit. A non-zero
exit status means the interface failed to load. This is what the packaged
self-test runs and what a clean-install check calls.
.TP
.B \-\-screenshot \fIPATH\fR
Save a window screenshot to
.I PATH
and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-dark
Start with the dark theme.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the option summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B QT_QPA_PLATFORM
Set to
.B offscreen
to run
.B \-\-self\-test
without a display server. This is how the packaging tests exercise the
application in a build environment that has no session.
.TP
.B OFI_PARSER_WORKER
Overrides the path to the parser worker. Honoured only in debug builds, so a
release binary always uses the worker installed beside it. It exists so tests
can point the dispatcher at a build artifact.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/openforensic-imager/libnative_bindings.so
The native core: evidence readers, hashing, the case database, and the audit
log.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/ofi-parser-worker
The isolated parser worker.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ofi-parser-worker (8)
.SH DOCUMENTATION
.I /usr/share/doc/openforensic-imager/SECURITY_MODEL.md
describes the trust boundaries, including what the parser worker sandbox does
and does not protect against.
.I /usr/share/doc/openforensic-imager/THIRD_PARTY.md
lists the third-party components and their licenses.
